	ViewData["Title"] = "Dakar 1998";
	Layout = "~/Pages/Shared/_Layout.cshtml";
}

	<div class="text-center">
		<h1>
			Summary (1998 - Paris - Gao - Dakar)
		</h1>
		<p>
			<img src="1998_map.jpg" alt="" style="float:left;" />
			For the second year in a row, Mitsubishi dominated the Paris-Dakar Rally
			and filled the first four places of the overall standings with its machines. Mitsubishi
			also managed to win the marathon classification for production-based vehicles
			once again at the 20th running of the rally. After many luckless attempts, the
			Frenchman Jean-Pierre Fontenay succeeded this year in winning the rally for himself,
			taking victory in his Off-Road Express Mitsubishi Ralliart Pajero. Everyone agreed
			that this Dakar was one of the toughest anyone could remember, for it
			<img src="1998_01.jpg" alt="" style="float:right;" />
			led over 10,000 km through the African continent. For 18 days, the victory was
			fought out over 6,338 km of racing between Paris and Senegal, with the rally
			caravan crossing France, Spain, Morocco, Mauritania, Mali and
			Senegal. The worst part was the massive three marathon days, in which the
			cars, trucks and motorcycles had to cross the toughest terrain in the world and
			were not allowed to accept any help from their flying technicians. From the start,
			as the rally led over relatively short special stages in Europe, it became clear
			that the Mitsubishis would dominate this year's running of the Dakar. When the
			rally then crossed the Mediterranean and continued in Morocco,
			<img src="1998_02.jpg" alt="" style="float:left;" />
			the French team PIAA Mitsubishi with its drivers Bruno Saby and Dominique
			Serieys demonstrated the full potential of the Pajero and extended its lead over
			the entire field considerably. Although Saby was put under pressure by his teammates
			Shinozuka and Fontenay, he kept the lead until the rally met the sand of the Sahara
			at the southern border of Morocco and the first marathon sections began.
			Shinozuka and Saby suffered from gearbox problems as they travelled the old trading route
			between Morocco and Timbuktu. They were on their way to the start of the first
			marathon section in the town of Zouerat in the heart of the Sahara. On this stretch,
			Fontenay took the lead, which he did not give up again until the finish. As the motorised
			<img src="1998_03.jpg" alt="" style="float:right;" />
			competition had to struggle with technical problems, the terrain alone remained the challenger
			for the Mitsubishis. Near the town of Gao, Shinozuka then dropped back to fourth
			place after digging himself into the soft dune sand, while Saby and
			Fontenay, separated by only minutes, fought a thrilling head-to-head race.
			It was a historic battle and only 10 minutes lay between Saby and Fontenay,
			while the rest of the field had fallen more than an hour behind. After a
			sprint of just 18 km across the beaches of the Pink Lake, near Dakar, Fontenay's
			Pajero shot over the finish ramp first of the whole rally caravan to the applause
			of thousands of spectators. It was a happy moment for Fontenay and he dedicated the victory
			to his long-time navigator and friend Bruno Mussmarra. With the victories of the previous year
			and the outstanding performance at this Dakar, Mitsubishi had once again proven
			that the Pajero was the king of the desert.
		</p>
